I'm considering the concept of cowardice and whether it really means refusing to fight. Some traditions link courage with fighting, but not always explicitly with cowardice. In cases where fighting is impossible, not acting could be seen as courage instead. I wonder if we can categorize certain actions as vices, like passive complicity, which isn't clearly defined in existing lists. Child sacrifice could definitely be viewed as an injustice in many traditions emphasizing care and compassion. So maybe we can frame cowardice as a form of acquiescence.**Assessing vices and traditions**

